**Common Patterns Across Competitors

* Clear hero sections are commonly used to introduce employability or work-experience support
* Strong calls to action are used to encourage users to explore opportunities, register interest or access further support
* Real imagery of young people and workplace environments is frequently used to make programmes feel more relatable
* Most competitors target young people aged approximately 16–30
* Work experience is consistently presented as a way to overcome the barrier of having limited previous employment experience
* Confidence building is a recurring benefit across employability programmes
* Transferable skills such as communication, teamwork and time management are commonly promoted
* CV creation and improvement is a common form of employment support
* Interview preparation is regularly included within employability resources
* Real participant stories and outcomes are used to build trust and demonstrate programme value
* Recognised employers and partner organisations are used as trust signals
* Opportunity or job-search functionality is commonly provided
* Location information is important when presenting employment opportunities
* FAQs are frequently used to answer common questions and reduce uncertainty
* Mobile-responsive layouts are used across all competitors
* Cards, imagery and separated content sections are commonly used to reduce large blocks of text
* Most competitors provide multiple routes to additional support or resources
* Programme benefits are usually communicated more clearly than the actual programme journey
* Few competitors provide a clear visual timeline showing exactly what happens during a placement
* Transferable skills are often mentioned without clearly explaining how participants develop them
* Websites frequently serve multiple audiences, such as candidates, employers and partners, which can weaken the focus of the user journey
* Large navigation menus and extensive content are common and can create information overload
* Mobile experiences often require significant scrolling
* Important calls to action can become less prominent when surrounded by large amounts of content
* Local travel information is rarely integrated directly with employment opportunities
* There is limited personalisation for users who do not know which career, sector or opportunity is suitable for them
* Interactive learning features such as quizzes exist on some platforms but are not commonly integrated throughout the full employability journey
* Users are often required to navigate across several pages to understand the full programme and its benefits
* Accessibility is considered to varying degrees but is not always consistently reflected across individual pages
